WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.941 --> 00:00:03.524
(upbeat music)

00:00:06.840 --> 00:00:08.291
- I remember the first time we got on

00:00:08.291 --> 00:00:11.277
a start line, and it’s just adrenaline.

00:00:11.277 --> 00:00:15.359
Blood was pumping, heart rate was racing.

00:00:15.359 --> 00:00:17.708
Being on a sidecar does
add that extra dimension

00:00:17.708 --> 00:00:21.766
because you've got to work
with the person next to you.

00:00:21.766 --> 00:00:24.660
Having him as my brother in a sidecar

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:27.124
is what it's all about.

00:00:27.124 --> 00:00:28.135
- I'm Sam Houghton.

00:00:28.135 --> 00:00:29.032
- I'm Andy Houghton.

00:00:29.032 --> 00:00:29.865
- We're brothers.

00:00:29.865 --> 00:00:33.615
- And we're a competitive
sidecarcross team.

00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:39.340
I started racing probably
when I was about 10.

00:00:39.340 --> 00:00:42.167
I'd been on a bike since four years old.

00:00:42.167 --> 00:00:44.535
Oh there you go that's
a mean look isn't it?

00:00:44.535 --> 00:00:47.118
I was maybe 13 in that picture.

00:00:48.183 --> 00:00:49.601
Sam beat me once.

00:00:49.601 --> 00:00:50.753
- In the last race that we had.

00:00:50.753 --> 00:00:51.586
- No yeah, yeah

00:00:51.586 --> 00:00:52.419
- [Sam] Well, you know
- But you still only,

00:00:52.419 --> 00:00:53.252
- [Sam] as your last race.
- you still only,

00:00:53.252 --> 00:00:54.571
still ever only beaten me once.

00:00:54.571 --> 00:00:55.654
- [Sam] Yeah.

00:00:57.052 --> 00:00:59.495
- Won my first championship
with the local club.

00:00:59.495 --> 00:01:01.361
About 11 or 12 years old, rode my bike

00:01:01.361 --> 00:01:03.836
all the way up through
different championships

00:01:03.836 --> 00:01:08.003
till I was 24 in 2005 and
that's when I had my accident.

00:01:13.383 --> 00:01:17.115
I had an accident while
I was out training.

00:01:17.115 --> 00:01:18.609
I ended up going over the handle bars and,

00:01:18.609 --> 00:01:23.548
and landing flat on my back,
shattered my T7 vertebrae.

00:01:23.548 --> 00:01:26.881
That one moment kind of changed my life.

00:01:28.135 --> 00:01:30.047
I came out of hospital
and then I spent a couple

00:01:30.047 --> 00:01:32.425
of years without, without doing much.

00:01:32.425 --> 00:01:34.239
There’s only so long that you can fend off

00:01:34.239 --> 00:01:38.217
that desire and that bug
to get back on a bike.

00:01:38.217 --> 00:01:39.487
We found a sidecar and now I'm out

00:01:39.487 --> 00:01:42.654
racing this thing, which is fantastic.

00:01:45.183 --> 00:01:47.284
When I tell people that I race sidecar

00:01:47.284 --> 00:01:48.605
they seem to assume that I'm gonna

00:01:48.605 --> 00:01:50.623
be the one in the passenger side but

00:01:50.623 --> 00:01:53.949
that's not the case, I'm on the bike.

00:01:53.949 --> 00:01:56.205
The guys in the sidecar,
there's no sitting.

00:01:56.205 --> 00:01:58.157
There’s no sitting at all,
they're throwing themselves

00:01:58.157 --> 00:02:02.324
from left to right and you've
gotta be fully able-bodied.

00:02:03.544 --> 00:02:04.962
- My role on the team is effectively

00:02:04.962 --> 00:02:07.051
to turn the bike and to keep it level.

00:02:07.051 --> 00:02:08.472
To do that it's a case of chucking

00:02:08.472 --> 00:02:10.648
your weight from side to side.

00:02:10.648 --> 00:02:12.259
If Andrew wants to go one way,

00:02:12.259 --> 00:02:13.976
and I'm not on the same wavelength,

00:02:13.976 --> 00:02:16.408
we're not going that way.

00:02:16.408 --> 00:02:18.528
- Most of the time he knows what I'm gonna do.

00:02:18.528 --> 00:02:20.195
As brothers you've probably got that,

00:02:20.195 --> 00:02:21.099
that relationship a little bit

00:02:21.099 --> 00:02:23.766
closer than you would otherwise.

00:02:26.712 --> 00:02:29.287
That feeling you get
when you're out on the track,

00:02:29.287 --> 00:02:32.112
for me it's, it's what I live for.

00:02:32.112 --> 00:02:34.010
Keep, you get it banked into a corner

00:02:34.010 --> 00:02:37.595
and you've got the bike drifting sideways.

00:02:37.595 --> 00:02:40.000
I'm out there and no one knows
that I'm in a wheelchair.

00:02:40.000 --> 00:02:41.547
No one knows.

00:02:41.547 --> 00:02:43.956
You're just one of the guys,
you're a sidecar racer.

00:02:43.956 --> 00:02:45.675
You're not a disabled guy,

00:02:45.675 --> 00:02:48.508
and it just is an amazing feeling.

